messages:
Alex H.: Hey team — running into a weird streaming failure in our Edge deployment. After a token rotation the client ReadableStream just stops emitting chunks and eventually throws a closed-stream error. Happening on Node 18 edge runtime and our service uses the TypeScript SDK. Repro steps below.

Alex H.: repro (simplified):
```js
// serverless edge handler
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_API_KEY });

export default async function handler(req) {
  const stream = await client.chat.stream({ model: 'open-light-1', messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'hello' }] });
  const reader = stream.getReader();
  while (true) {
    const { value, done } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    // forward chunk to response
  }
}
```

Alex H.: logs around failure: "stream closed unexpectedly: 401 Unauthorized" then later "ReadableStreamDefaultReader cancelled". If we refresh the request after rotation it works fine. :confused:

Priya R.: Thanks Alex — do you know the SDK version and whether you use automatic token refresh? Also is your rotation returning 401s mid-stream or does the backend send a 401 then a retry?

Alex H.: SDK is 0.9.2, token rotation via our internal auth proxy that swaps the Authorization header and returns 401 when invalid. The 401 comes mid-stream (we can see one chunk then 401).

Marco L.: This sounds similar to a bug we fixed in the SDK where mid-stream 401s caused the internal reader to cancel without propagating a recoverable error. Can you confirm Node runtime is the 'edge' target (no global fetch polyfill)? Also any reverse proxies in front?

Alex H.: Yes edge target (Vercel Edge). Proxy is our auth-proxy (header swap), nothing else.

Marco L.: Repro notes for support: what happens is: SSE/streaming connection remains, then auth-proxy returns an HTTP 401 on the same connection chunk. The SDK was treating any non-200 mid-stream event as a terminal close and calling reader.cancel(), which on Edge throws a closed error and prevents consumers from gracefully handling a reconnect.

Marco L.: Quick workaround: pin SDK to 0.9.3 (patch) — we released a hotfix where the reader emits an error event instead of cancelling immediately, allowing your handler to catch and either reconnect or surface a nicer error. Example handling change:
```js
const reader = stream.getReader();
try {
  for (;;) {
    const { value, done } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    // process chunk
  }
} catch (err) {
  // err will now be a stream error you can inspect and trigger a reconnect
}
```

Kelly S.: Also confirm you aren't using an older Node fetch polyfill that swaps streaming semantics. For Vercel Edge make sure you use the SDK build for edge (import from '@redwood/inference/edge' if you have both builds). If you import the Node build into an Edge runtime it can cause subtle ReadableStream mismatches.

Alex H.: Oh — we were importing the top-level package in one route (serverless edge) and the edge-specific package in others. That might explain the inconsistent behavior. Will try the patched SDK + consistent edge import.

Priya R.: If you can, capture a HAR or the exact bytes around the 401. The SDK now logs a debug line on 0.9.3: `STREAM: mid-stream status 401, emitting error` which helps. You can enable debug via `RW_DEBUG=stream` env var.

Support Bot: Deploy bot noticed a new release: SDK 0.9.3 published 2059002440. Release notes: "streaming: handle mid-stream non-2xx as non-terminal error; edge import guidance; fix Reader cancel propagation". Link: https://pkg.registry/redwood/sdk/0.9.3

Alex H.: Installed 0.9.3, normalized imports to `@redwood/inference/edge`, enabled RW_DEBUG, and re-ran the repro. Now I get: `STREAM: mid-stream status 401, emitting error` then my catch block logs `StreamError: 401 during stream, auth-rotation` — I can now trigger a reconnect and resume. :tada:

Marco L.: Great — can you keep an eye for any similar 403s or 5xx mid-stream returns? For 5xx we still close the stream because payloads can be corrupted. If you see repeated mid-stream auth failures, recommend making token swap atomic before starting streams.

Alex H.: Will do. Thanks for the quick turnaround and the patch!

Kelly S.: Filing a short internal postmortem (eng-runtime) to add better guidance on imports and token rotation best practices in the docs.

Priya R.: Marking this resolved for now — follow up if anything else crops up. :thumbsup:
